{
  "dom_id": "step-q87144",
  "name": "Question 8",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14215498/assets/41cd1216273c3157628ffb6879f50bc41dbed5490b310f5247914b2d47e6500c.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the function $y=f(x).$ Which of the following gives the graph of $y=4f(\\frac{1}{3}x)?$",
  "worked_solution": "To perform transformations of functions with multiple operations, we must remember the following rule:\n\n1\\. First, we perform the transformations *inside* the argument of $f.$ We first carry out horizontal stretches, followed by horizontal translations.\n\n2\\. Then, we perform the transformations *outside* the argument of $f.$ We first carry out vertical stretches, followed by vertical translations.\n\nTo plot $y=4f(\\frac{1}{3}x),$ we apply the following steps:\n\n- Take the curve $y=f(x).$\n\n- Stretch it by a scale factor of $\\frac{1}{(\\frac{1}{3})}=3$ parallel to the $x$-axis to get the graph of $y=f(\\frac{1}{3}x).$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14215498/assets/a4ac07da53b80c9ce828e9adca5efc41635d2ca4d56b97340aafa09c824bf956.png)\n\n- Then, stretch it by a scale factor of $4$ parallel to the $y$-axis to get the graph of $y=4f(\\frac{1}{3}x).$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14215498/assets/28fc05b5ed03768282704609a1d4c05e5c579e75bca0bd5c9f4776c460fe8ae1.png)",
